  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nCurrently, low clouds are continuing to fill in along the coastal \nareas of San Diego and Orange counties, with very little eastward \nextent into the inland areas. This will likely continue to be the \ncase, with weak offshore flow in place. Temperatures remain \nrelatively mild at this hour, with upper 60s to low 70s being felt \nfor most of the inland and coastal locations. These clouds should \nmix out and clear will likely occur for most of the inland areas \nthrough 8 AM. \n\nThere is going to be an influx of some mid level moisture from the \nsoutheast by later in the day, which may allow for a shower or \npossibly a shower to develop over the mountains by later this \nafternoon. Given that there will be a better surge of moisture and \nslightly better SB-CAPE values in place on Sunday, there will be a \nbetter chance of a few showers and/or thunderstorms developing over \nthe mountains during the afternoon on Sunday. With the ridge only \nweakening and shifting eastward slightly through the weekend, hot \ntemperatures will remain in place, especially for the Inland \nEmpire, with little relief in the cooling of temperatures overnight. \n\nMonday through Friday...\n\nAs the upper level ridge over the inner mountain west does shift a \nlittle further east through the early part of the week, with will \nallow for there to be a gradual cooldown and decrease in humidity \nvalues through Wednesday. This will be followed by an increase in \ntemperatures, with a return to being anywhere from 10 to 15 degrees \nabove the seasonal average towards the end of end week as the ridge \nbegins to retrograde back to the west and strengthen, along with \n600 dm heights over our desert regions by Thursday. There will \nalso be better propagation of monsoonal waves advecting moisture \nin over the region, which will help to increase chances of \nafternoon thunderstorms for the mountains and deserts.",
